   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marker</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator ascending="1" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project elements pay attention to climate adaptation</narrative>
    </title>
    <reference code="M" indicator-uri="https://www.rvo.nl/sites/default/files/rvo_website_content/opendata/iati/RVO Indicator Reference.ods" vocabulary="99"></reference>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Aim to go from curative to preventive measures related to better control of water quality of the fishponds</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Generally, in the aquaculture sector in Myanmar we see relatively large, open fishponds, which makes these 
ponds rather climate sensitive and difficult to manage. This year, we saw a relatively heavy rainy season. 
Heavy rains can cause big variations of chemistry in the water and sudden temperature drops, which 
increase the stress level of fish and makes them less resistant to diseases. Heavy rains also increase the risk 
of runoff from nearby agricultural land potentially leading to fertilisers and pesticides contaminating the 
ponds. Most Myanmar fish farmers tend to go for typical curative measures (usages of antibiotics and 
chemicals) to address its consequences, which was confirmed by our baseline study. Therefore, one of the 
key themes identified for the PAP Production Guidelines and training is the transition from using curative
measures to preventive measures, i.e., the better control of the water quality of the fishponds. 
Another related issue to be addressed the coming months is the periods of saline water in the Khayan area, 
which was identified during the hatchery assessment at Golden Fish World. Alpha Aqua and the PAP team 
have identified that salinity sensors will need to be installed at Golden Fish World&#x2019;s farm, with an automatic 
switch so the water source for the hatchery will be automatically switched from the river to the tube well 
when salinity levels are too high. These sensors will probably be installed in the river (and not directly on 
Golden Fish World&#x2019;s farm), so that a general warning can be issued to other fish farmers in the Khayan area 
as well.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marker</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator ascending="1" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project is gender aware</narrative>
    </title>
    <reference code="M" indicator-uri="https://www.rvo.nl/sites/default/files/rvo_website_content/opendata/iati/RVO Indicator Reference.ods" vocabulary="99"></reference>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender aware: project partner level and target group level</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1           The gender strategy of the project is twofold: first, one directed at project partner level (consortium) and 
secondly a gender strategy directed at target group level: fish/hatchery farmers and workers. At the project 
partner level, we generally have a good gender balance with about 50% of those involved in PAP being 
female. On the target group level, however, the baseline clearly pointed out that fish farming is a maledominated activity. 97% of the ones in charge of a fish farm were male, while women generally play more 
supportive roles. Of the workers on the farms, female workers are completely absent from full-time 
positions, but represent about 10.75% of the seasonal workers. Generally, they usually perform the tasks of 
recording the biomass harvested and sorting of the fish by size.
As a temporary project, we think that we cannot change the cultural or economic reasons for females not 
being owners of the fish farms, but we will at least focus on their existing tasks as seasonal workers. In our
training curriculum development, we need to dive deeper into their specific training needs, but also assess 
whether they would be interested in a more diverse set of tasks or if they could play a role in newly to-bentroduced tasks, such as farm business management and record keeping. Year 4 will start with Scheme 1: 
Farm Management of the training curriculum, which will enable us to look at a higher level at fish farm 
management, how roles are traditionally divided and how we can support the roles of women better. We will 
also make sure that our trainer teams will always consist of at least one female to make them feel 
Progress report 
comfortable enough to express their specific needs. Our current team of 25 extension staff consists of 9
female trainers.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1  No change in strategy, see comment year before</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1 same strategy</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1 same strategy</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
