   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Circular Economy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marker</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator ascending="1" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project elements take into account CE principles</narrative>
    </title>
    <reference code="M" indicator-uri="https://www.rvo.nl/sites/default/files/rvo_website_content/opendata/iati/RVO Indicator Reference.ods" vocabulary="99"></reference>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maximizing feed-food conversion</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-07-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-08-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">By analysing the nutrient flow of a fish farm, the most distinctive input is the fish feed. In aquaculture, a 
major concern has been the challenge of feeding farmed fish with products that are nutritious and at the 
same time economically and environmentally sustainable. One of the major components of PAP therefore 
was the set-up of the first fingerling feedline in Myanmar by De Heus, which has been completed in the past
years. This fingerling feedline produces extruded pelleted (floating) feed. Generally, the extrusion of feed is 
a process that results in that starch and proteins are more digestible, and leads to better water stability and 
physical integrity, therefore maximizing food-conversion ratio and limiting pollution. The ability to produce 
floating pellet and balanced micro-diets allows for more efficient feeding management. The benefits of using 
different types of feed will also be demonstrated to farmers at the AAC when we start the feeding pilots in 
September.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-08-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-08-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
